1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:00,649
Fair.

2
00:00:00,649 --> 00:00:05,247
Everything so far in this section has been judgement: choosing cases, wording

3
00:00:05,247 --> 00:00:10,143
questions, checking agreement, and all of that stays real work a spreadsheet can't

4
00:00:10,143 --> 00:00:10,800
do for you.

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,607
But the table you tallied by hand last lecture?

6
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:15,219
That part can tally itself.

7
00:00:15,219 --> 00:00:19,698
This is the lecture where you finally build something that runs on its own.

8
00:00:19,698 --> 00:00:21,250
You need exactly two tabs.

9
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:24,117
One where you enter scores, one that reads them.

10
00:00:24,117 --> 00:00:28,595
No macros, no plugins, nothing beyond formulas you've probably used before.

11
00:00:28,595 --> 00:00:33,432
By the end of this you'll have a working version of the exact breakdown from last

12
00:00:33,432 --> 00:00:38,448
lecture, the one that caught a bucket collapsing under a flat aggregate, except this

13
00:00:38,448 --> 00:00:43,464
one recalculates the moment you type a new score in, instead of you doing it by hand

14
00:00:43,464 --> 00:00:45,912
on a Sunday night before a launch review.

15
00:00:45,912 --> 00:00:50,749
If you've built a pivot table before, you already have every skill this requires.

16
00:00:50,749 --> 00:00:53,138
Nothing here is more advanced than that.

17
00:00:53,688 --> 00:00:58,694
Here's tab one, and it's almost exactly the two-column sheet from the calibration

18
00:00:58,694 --> 00:01:00,486
lecture, just made permanent.

19
00:01:00,486 --> 00:01:01,536
One row per case.

20
00:01:01,536 --> 00:01:03,761
A case ID, so you can find it again.

21
00:01:03,761 --> 00:01:06,851
The bucket it belongs to, from the four you built.

22
00:01:06,851 --> 00:01:11,918
Then six columns, one per rubric question, one for pass and zero for fail, entered

23
00:01:11,918 --> 00:01:14,019
by you or by your validated judge.

24
00:01:14,019 --> 00:01:19,025
Case C027 there is a shape you'll see constantly: five questions pass, one fails,

25
00:01:19,025 --> 00:01:20,755
question three specifically.

26
00:01:20,755 --> 00:01:23,289
That's not a rounding error to wave away.

27
00:01:23,289 --> 00:01:28,109
It's the exact case you'd pull up when someone asks why the adversarial bucket

28
00:01:28,109 --> 00:01:28,603
dropped.

29
00:01:28,603 --> 00:01:30,148
Fifty rows, nine columns.

30
00:01:30,148 --> 00:01:31,508
That's the entire tab.

31
00:01:31,508 --> 00:01:36,513
Nothing about scale here is the hard part; the hard part happened already, in the

32
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:40,530
two lectures that taught you what belongs in columns C through H.

33
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:43,016
Two formulas, and you're done.

34
00:01:43,016 --> 00:01:46,372
The first lives in the Scores tab, one copy per row.

35
00:01:46,372 --> 00:01:51,600
It adds up the six pass or fail columns, and if all six read one, the case passes

36
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:52,116
overall.

37
00:01:52,116 --> 00:01:55,085
Miss a single question and the row reads zero.

38
00:01:55,085 --> 00:02:00,313
No partial credit, on purpose: the rubric said pass or fail, so the overall score

39
00:02:00,313 --> 00:02:01,216
respects that.

40
00:02:01,216 --> 00:02:04,766
The second lives on the Dashboard tab, once per bucket.

41
00:02:04,766 --> 00:02:10,058
It's an average-if: average the overall column, but only for rows where the bucket

42
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:12,446
matches, common path in this example.

43
00:02:12,446 --> 00:02:17,481
Since overall is just ones and zeros, averaging them is exactly the pass rate.

44
00:02:17,481 --> 00:02:22,386
Same formula, four times, once per bucket, and you have last lecture's table

45
00:02:22,386 --> 00:02:23,483
computing itself.

46
00:02:23,483 --> 00:02:28,646
Change one bucket name in the criteria and the same formula works for any of the

47
00:02:28,646 --> 00:02:28,969
four.

48
00:02:28,969 --> 00:02:34,390
You are not writing four different formulas, you're writing one formula and changing

49
00:02:34,390 --> 00:02:35,745
one word, four times.

50
00:02:36,295 --> 00:02:41,135
Here's the actual sequence, because "build a spreadsheet" undersells how little of

51
00:02:41,135 --> 00:02:42,610
this is spreadsheet work.

52
00:02:42,610 --> 00:02:47,214
Duplicate the template rather than starting blank, so the formulas are already

53
00:02:47,214 --> 00:02:49,043
sitting there waiting for data.

54
00:02:49,043 --> 00:02:53,293
Paste your case IDs and their buckets into the Scores tab, one row each.

55
00:02:53,293 --> 00:02:58,132
Then score every case: six ones or zeros, straight from the rubric you calibrated.

56
00:02:58,132 --> 00:03:00,434
Once that's filled in, you're finished.

57
00:03:00,434 --> 00:03:05,097
Overall and the Dashboard tab were already computing themselves the whole time.

58
00:03:05,097 --> 00:03:08,343
Notice where almost all the real time goes: step three.

59
00:03:08,343 --> 00:03:11,235
The sheet itself takes fifteen minutes to set up.

60
00:03:11,235 --> 00:03:16,192
Scoring fifty cases against a rubric is the same afternoon of work from two lectures

61
00:03:16,192 --> 00:03:19,556
ago, and no formula in this sheet makes that part faster.

62
00:03:19,556 --> 00:03:20,855
It was never meant to.

63
00:03:21,405 --> 00:03:25,821
Here's the payoff, and it's smaller than people expect, which is the point.

64
00:03:25,821 --> 00:03:28,177
Two formulas, written once, copied down.

65
00:03:28,177 --> 00:03:31,239
Zero manual retallying, ever again, for this rubric.

66
00:03:31,239 --> 00:03:36,068
And the dashboard updates the instant you finish entering one score, which matters

67
00:03:36,068 --> 00:03:41,015
more than it sounds: it means the four-bucket table from last lecture is live during

68
00:03:41,015 --> 00:03:45,903
the meeting where someone asks "what if we pulled that one case", not something you

69
00:03:45,903 --> 00:03:47,788
promise to rerun and send later.

70
00:03:47,788 --> 00:03:52,735
One more thing this buys you almost for free: sort the Scores tab by Overall, filter

71
00:03:52,735 --> 00:03:57,681
to zero, filter again to one bucket, and you're looking at exactly the failing cases

72
00:03:57,681 --> 00:04:00,803
behind whatever number the dashboard just showed you.

73
00:04:00,803 --> 00:04:05,632
That's the "go read those cases" step from last lecture's release gate, and it's a

74
00:04:05,632 --> 00:04:07,457
two-click filter, not a search.

75
00:04:08,007 --> 00:04:09,321
Say the cost plainly.

76
00:04:09,321 --> 00:04:11,887
This sheet is bookkeeping, not judgement.

77
00:04:11,887 --> 00:04:15,203
Nothing here writes the pass or fail columns for you.

78
00:04:15,203 --> 00:04:19,958
That's still the calibrated rubric and the validated judge from the last two

79
00:04:19,958 --> 00:04:23,211
lectures, doing the actual work, one case at a time.

80
00:04:23,211 --> 00:04:24,525
And it has a ceiling.

81
00:04:24,525 --> 00:04:29,406
Past a few hundred rows, formulas scanning whole columns get sluggish, and the

82
00:04:29,406 --> 00:04:34,474
failure mode is quiet: type "Common Path" with a capital P in one row, when every

83
00:04:34,474 --> 00:04:39,729
other row says "Common path," and that single case silently vanishes from the bucket

84
00:04:39,729 --> 00:04:40,230
average.

85
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:41,544
Nobody gets an error.

86
00:04:41,544 --> 00:04:46,487
The number is just wrong, and it looks exactly as confident as the correct one.

87
00:04:47,037 --> 00:04:50,394
Notice the definition doesn't mention spreadsheets at all.

88
00:04:50,394 --> 00:04:55,256
A harness is anything that does this reliably, and a spreadsheet is just the version

89
00:04:55,256 --> 00:04:56,992
with the lowest cost to start.

90
00:04:56,992 --> 00:05:01,449
The exercise deliberately reuses your fifteen calibration cases, the ones you

91
00:05:01,449 --> 00:05:04,574
already hand-scored twice, back in the rubric lecture.

92
00:05:04,574 --> 00:05:06,600
You know what the answer should be.

93
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:11,346
Build the sheet against cases where you can already check its homework, before you

94
00:05:11,346 --> 00:05:15,224
trust it with the other thirty-five you haven't scored by hand yet.

95
00:05:15,224 --> 00:05:18,523
This is also the cheapest way to catch a formula mistake.

96
00:05:18,523 --> 00:05:23,385
If your hand tally said sixty percent and the sheet says ninety, the sheet is wrong,

97
00:05:23,385 --> 00:05:28,132
not your feature, and you'll want to know that before it's the number in a release

98
00:05:28,132 --> 00:05:29,868
review instead of a rehearsal.

99
00:05:30,418 --> 00:05:32,225
One thing before you move on.

100
00:05:32,225 --> 00:05:37,336
The working spreadsheet, both tabs, both formulas, already built, is A03, attached

101
00:05:37,336 --> 00:05:38,333
to this lecture.

102
00:05:38,333 --> 00:05:43,569
Duplicate it rather than starting from a blank sheet, enter your fifteen calibration

103
00:05:43,569 --> 00:05:48,679
cases first, and check the dashboard against the number you already know is right.

104
00:05:48,679 --> 00:05:53,915
Once it matches, add the rest of your fifty with confidence, and keep the sheet open

105
00:05:53,915 --> 00:05:55,722
every release after this one.

106
00:05:55,722 --> 00:06:00,708
Next lecture, we build the identical harness again, this time in thirty lines of

107
00:06:00,708 --> 00:06:05,694
Python, so it can run itself automatically every time someone proposes a change,

108
00:06:05,694 --> 00:06:08,748
instead of waiting for you to open a spreadsheet.
